## 1. What was done

For two fixed inputs (A: the tracked acceptance fixture; B: a new
closure-lazy fixture built for this task) the matrix measures each
obligation-3 property at the layer that can actually observe it, using:

- L1 generation: `haxe <driver>.hxml` exit code + generated file list
- L2 syntax: `swiftc -typecheck` (whole module)
- L3 build: `swiftc -c` (whole module; **not** `-typecheck` — the
  recorded trap, and §5 below measures the trap on this candidate)
- L4 behaviour: `swiftc -o` build, run the compiled binary, compare its
  stdout line-for-line against the Haxe interpreter oracle
- L5 discrimination: a deliberately-broken backend variant per property;
  the check MUST fail on it (LAYERED-VERIFICATION L5; six demonstrated,
  the rule requires ≥ 2)

Generation succeeded (L1), typechecks (L2), builds (L3) and runs
correctly (L4) are four distinct strengths; each is reported separately.

